  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 203 PM EDT Fri Aug 28 2026\n\nAfternoon GOES satellite imagery reveals blossoming diurnal cu \nacross the UP with two distinct lake breezes in the east half. The \ncollision of these two boundaries may be the focal point for a few \nscattered showers, however, soundings in the east reveal a low-level \ninverted v below a very shallow moist layer, which is then topped \noff by a strong mid-level cap, so overall not thinking anything more \nthan a few showers (15-20% chance). Otherwise, pleasant weather \ncontinues through the rest of the day with high temps poking into \nthe low to mid 70s, 60s north. tonight, patchy dense fog is possible \n(40-50% chance) across areas of the interior under mostly clear \nskies and diminishing winds. Overnight lows fall into the upper 40s \nto mid 50s.\n\nMore widespread precipitation chances return tomorrow PM as midlevel \nflow becomes more zonal, allowing transient shortwaves to return to \nthe Great Lakes. Isentropic lift ahead of a mid-level shortwave \nmoving through Wisconsin tomorrow will be the forcing mechanism for \nareas of showers and perhaps a few thunderstorms, mainly across the \nsouth half of the UP. NBM probability of widespread wetting rain \ndoesn't give much confidence in mitigating ongoing drought conditions \nwith only a 40-60% chance for amounts >0.25\" in the south half, \nlower in the northern half and Keweenaw. Additionally, CAM guidance \nisn't in full agreement in the overall placement of precip. \nRAP/HRRR/RRFS 12z runs place the bulk of rain in WI. Regardless, any \nrain is welcomed! Additional rounds of showers are possible (25-50%) \nSunday PM through Monday as embedded shortwaves within broad \nsouthwesterly flow aloft work across the region.",
